[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
I could not reproduce an inter‑campus outage. End‑to‑end testing shows cross‑campus reachability is healthy from every LAN I probed. The topology‑wide check returned 0% loss between “here” subnets (10.5.0.0/24, 10.6.0.0/24) and the “other campus” subnets (172.16.10/20/30). For example, pc1 (10.5.0.1) successfully pinged pc13 (172.16.30.13) and multiple others with normal RTTs. Gateways on both sides are up: sshswitch has 10.5.0.254 and 10.6.0.254 up; mswitch0 has 172.16.{10,20,30}.254 up. 

Routing is converged and redundant. All core routers (router7/8/10/11/16/17) show OSPF adjacencies in Full state and have full remote route coverage. For instance, router16 learns 172.16.10/20/30 via mswitch0 (10.2.2.2) and the rest of the fabric via 172.30.1.2; router17 has ECMP to the opposite campus prefixes via 172.29.10.1 and 192.168.144.1; router7 likewise has Full neighbors on all interconnects. Inter‑campus bonds are UP (sshswitch bond0, router17 bond0) and carrying the expected VLANs; OSPF neighbor over 55.5.1.x is Full.

Given this direct evidence, there is no current fault in the inter‑campus device or links. The earlier report may have been a transient event that self‑cleared. No corrective action is required now. If it recurs, please capture timestamps and a traceroute from an affected host; we can also add monitoring on the inter‑campus bond (sshswitch bond0 ↔ router17 bond0) to detect flaps. 
=== END ===